# nr-tui-launch-update: reduced by the wire audit (t19). Already applied: the mixdog-launch dependencies (mixdog-ai, mixdog-core-domains, reqwest, sha2, tokio, zip) and their lock entries; the entry calls perform_pending_swap() and register_live_session() (mixdog-entry/src/dispatch.rs).

## The entry (mixdog-entry dispatch.rs / main.rs: lead hot file). NOT applied, in this order as cli.mjs does:
- first of all, when argv[1] == mixdog_launch::staged_update::STAGED_INSTALL_ARG ("--staged-install-worker"): std::process::exit(run_staged_install_worker(&argv[2])) (the worker spawn_staged_install starts; no console and no window).
- when perform_pending_swap() returned SwapOutcome::Swapped(exe) and the process was not itself started by a swap: if let Some(code) = reexec_swapped(&exe, &original_args) { finish_process_lifecycle(..., "clean-shutdown" or, when a signal ended the child, "forced-cleanup", code); return code } (None: the run goes on in place). dispatch.rs today only tests the Swapped case and continues.
- where the update check finds a newer version and auto-update is on (self-update.mjs STAGE path): spawn_staged_install(&latest). None of run_staged_install_worker, spawn_staged_install, reexec_swapped has a caller outside mixdog-launch.
- The daemon's update_installer.rs stage half still runs npm and is retired by its own task; the two share the staging folder names, not a marker shape.

## Process steps
cargo hakari generate (workspace-hack), then D:\mixdog-build\tools\unify-test-features.py for mixdog-launch.

## Owner decision (D6, the desktop release feed)
staged_update::FEED_URL = https://github.com/tribgames/mixdog/releases/latest/download/mixdog-cli-manifest.json must exist as a release asset: { "version": "<x.y.z>", "assets": { "<os>-<arch>": { "url": "...zip", "sha256": "<64 hex>", "size": <bytes> } } } (os win32|darwin|linux, arch x64|arm64; each asset a deflate zip holding the mixdog (mixdog.exe) binary at its root or in one folder, answering mixdog --version with a line containing the version). Integrity is the manifest's SHA-256 and size over TLS; no signature check was added (none was decided; a new refusal is not allowed): if the owner decides on a signature it is checked in stage.rs fetch.
